 "conclusion": "The rating surprise is centred within each race (race-mean exactly 0), so it is not day-drift. With NO rating control, a jockey's later rides at a meeting show no difference (-0.02 to -0.05 kg) and random or reverse orderings show nothing; the +0.15 to +0.37 kg 'later rides go better' pattern appears only when the ABSOLUTE pre-race rating difference between the rides is controlled, and flips sign in reverse order. Mechanism: within a meeting, later rides tend to be on higher-rated horses in stronger races, and the race-relative surprise depends on a horse's rating RELATIVE to its field, not its absolute rating; controlling absolute rating across races of different class manufactures an order effect. Prize money does not explain it. Rule: with race-relative outcomes, control race-relative ratings (rating minus field mean) or use no rating control for within-meeting comparisons. Workload conclusion unchanged and stronger: no order or workload effect."
}